.TH IPHONE-MIRROR-SCREEN 1 "2026" "iphone-mirror-bridge" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
iphone-mirror-screen \- start or stop the iPhone screen-mirroring receiver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iphone-mirror-screen
[\fB\-\-start\fR|\fB\-\-stop\fR|\fB\-\-status\fR|\fB\-\-allow\-firewall\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Starts an AirPlay receiver (UxPlay) named "iPhone Mirror Bridge" for the
current desktop user. On the iPhone, open Control Center, tap Screen
Mirroring and choose it. Without arguments the command toggles: it starts the
receiver if stopped and stops it if running. The "iPhone Screen Mirroring"
application menu entry runs this command.
.PP
The iPhone and computer must be on the same network. The receiver uses
TCP ports 7000, 7001, 7100 and UDP ports 6000, 6001, 7011, plus mDNS
(UDP 5353); allow them if a firewall is enabled. If the receiver is listed
on the iPhone but connecting fails with "Unable to connect", a firewall is
blocking these ports.
.PP
.B \-\-allow\-firewall
lets the iPhone through Wild OS's firewall: it marks the current network as
Home and allows the ports above for devices on that network, through
Wild OS's own settings tool (asks for an administrator password). When the
receiver starts and that firewall would block it, a notification says so.
.PP
For low latency the receiver runs UxPlay with
.B \-vsync no \-fps 60
and, when available, the VA-API decoder with
.B compliance=flexible
(it otherwise holds several frames) and, on Wayland,
.BR waylandsink .
Set
.B IPHONE_MIRROR_VIDEO_ARGS
to replace these options. Wi-Fi power saving on the computer is the most
common remaining cause of lag.
.SH FILES
.I ~/.cache/iphone-mirror-bridge/uxplay.log
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR uxplay (1),
.BR iphone-mirror-bridge (1)
